    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: reject a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with a 400.\n\nI implemented it with a **single-file change** to `AdjustRegistrationDate.java` \u2014 the one step in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline that already finalizes the effective registration date. It's a single-method class (no surrounding methods), so its structural cost is minimal: WMC_other = 0, and the added guard is one `if`.\n\n```java\npublic void service(@Val Owner owner) throws MissingOwnerFieldsException {\n    LocalDate date = owner.getRegistrationDate();\n    if (date.isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {\n        throw new MissingOwnerFieldsException(List.of(\"registrationDate\"));\n    }\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nRather than introduce a new exception + handler + escalation YAML + pipeline wiring (which would multiply the cost across ~4 files), I reused the existing `MissingOwnerFieldsException`, whose handler already responds **400** with an `errors` array naming the offending field. The escalation is already wired, the check runs under the existing `transaction` governance, and the future-date guard is placed before the weekend-rollover logic so an out-of-range supplied date is rejected up front.\n\nStructural footprint: **1 file touched**, one method changed, one added `if`.",
